    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^2+y^2=2$, with no additional restrictions.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2+y^2&=(\\cos t+\\sin t)^2+(\\cos t-\\sin t)^2\\\\x^2+y^2&=2\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t\\\\x^2+y^2&=2\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\cos t=\\frac{x+y}{2},\\qquad\\sin t=\\frac{x-y}{2}.$$\nFor every point on $x^2+y^2=2$, these two values have squares summing to $1$, so a real value of $t$ exists. Thus the entire circle is described, with no additional restrictions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
